          "content": "```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\ntypedef struct Node {\n    int data;\n    struct Node *next;\n} Node;\n\nNode *reverse_list(Node *head) {\n    Node *prev = NULL;\n    Node *curr = head;\n\n    while (curr != NULL) {\n        Node *next = curr->next;  /* save the rest of the list */\n        curr->next = prev;        /* reverse this node's pointer */\n        prev = curr;              /* move prev forward */\n        curr = next;              /* move curr forward */\n    }\n\n    return prev;                  /* new head */\n}\n```\n\nExample usage:\n\n```c\nint main(void) {\n    Node *a = malloc(sizeof(Node));\n    Node *b = malloc(sizeof(Node));\n    Node *c = malloc(sizeof(Node));\n\n    a->data = 1; a->next = b;\n    b->data = 2; b->next = c;\n    c->data = 3; c->next = NULL;\n\n    Node *head = reverse_list(a);\n\n    for (Node *p = head; p != NULL; p = p->next) {\n        printf(\"%d \", p->data);\n    }\n    printf(\"\\n\");  /* prints: 3 2 1 */\n\n    free(head->next->next);\n    free(head->next);\n    free(head);\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Pointer changes for three nodes\n\nSuppose the list is:\n\n```text\nA -> B -> C -> NULL\n```\n\nInitial state:\n\n```text\nprev = NULL\ncurr = A\n```\n\n#### Step 1: `curr = A`\n\n```c\nnext = curr->next;  /* next = B */\ncurr->next = prev;  /* A->next = NULL */\nprev = curr;        /* prev = A */\ncurr = next;        /* curr = B */\n```\n\nAfter step 1:\n\n```text\nNULL <- A    B -> C -> NULL\n        prev curr\n```\n\n#### Step 2: `curr = B`\n\n```c\nnext = curr->next;  /* next = C */\ncurr->next = prev;  /* B->next = A */\nprev = curr;        /* prev = B */\ncurr = next;        /* curr = C */\n```\n\nAfter step 2:\n\n```text\nNULL <- A <- B    C -> NULL\n             prev curr\n```\n\n#### Step 3: `curr = C`\n\n```c\nnext = curr->next;  /* next = NULL */\ncurr->next = prev;  /* C->next = B */\nprev = curr;        /* prev = C */\ncurr = next;        /* curr = NULL */\n```\n\nAfter step 3:\n\n```text\nNULL <- A <- B <- C\n                  prev\n```\n\nLoop ends because `curr == NULL`. Return `prev`, which is `C`.\n\nFinal list:\n\n```text\nC -> B -> A -> NULL\n```\n\nThe key idea is that before changing `curr->next`, we save the next node in `next`. Then we reverse the current node’s pointer, move `prev` to the current node, and move `curr` to the saved next node. This reverses the list in place using `O(n)` time and `O(1)` extra space.",
